.\" Man page for photonicat-pm kernel driver
.TH PCAT-PM-CTL 4 "August 2026" "Linux" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
pcat-pm-ctl \- Photonicat power manager control device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/pcat-pm-ctl
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B pcat-pm-ctl
device provides a raw interface to communicate with the Photonicat 2
power management unit (PMU) over the serial protocol.
.PP
Most PMU features are accessible through dedicated sysfs attributes
or kernel interfaces (see below). This device serves as an escape
hatch for advanced use cases such as multi-entry scheduled boot.
.PP
Userspace writes a complete protocol frame; the kernel assigns a
frame number, recomputes the CRC, and forwards allowed commands to
the PMU. Selected PMU responses can be read back (supports
.BR poll (2)).
This includes PMU hardware and firmware version acknowledgment frames;
the driver still updates the corresponding sysfs attributes internally.
See
.I photonicat-pm.h
for the protocol frame format and command definitions.
.SS PMU Firmware Capability Policy
The driver treats firmware behavior as runtime-observed capability or quirk
detection, not as a static firmware-version allowlist or denylist.
.TP
.B RTC and scheduled boot
Start as
.BR pending-probe .
For ABI stability,
.I /dev/rtc0
remains registered, but RTC reads, set-time, alarms, and raw scheduled-boot
commands are blocked until the PMU reports three consecutive valid, advancing
RTC samples. Passing that probe promotes
.B pmu_rtc_capability
to
.BR enabled-probe .
.TP
.B Battery capacity
Follows the vendor driver policy. PMU protocol v2 status reports use the
PMU-reported SOC byte directly. Shorter status reports fall back to
voltage-derived OCV SOC from the device-tree battery profile.
.TP
.B Energy and fan
PMU protocol v2 energy fields and fan auto-speed reset are not trusted by
current driver releases.
.B energy_full
remains the static device-tree design capacity,
.B energy_now
is not exported, and fan auto-speed restoration requires the documented
workarounds.
.TP
.B Power-on mode
Starts as
.BR pending-probe .
The driver queries the PMU on load, and any answer promotes
.B pmu_power_on_mode_capability
to
.BR enabled-probe .
.B power_on_mode
reads
.B ENODATA
while the capability is pending.
.TP
.B Charge stop threshold
Starts as
.BR pending-probe .
The driver queries the PMU on load; a reply in the 50\-100 range caches the
value and promotes
.B pmu_charge_threshold_capability
to
.BR enabled-probe .
.B charge_control_end_threshold
reads
.B ENODATA
while the capability is pending. Writes are not gated on the probe: firmware
without support does not answer, so the write fails with
.BR ETIMEDOUT .
.PP
A capability that stays
.B pending-probe
means the running firmware did not answer the driver's probe for it.
Per-firmware results are recorded in the project wiki:
.PP
.RS
.nf
https://github.com/HackingGate/photonicat-pm/wiki/MCU\-Firmware\-Observed\-Behavior
.fi
.RE
.SS Power Supply
.TP
.B /sys/class/power_supply/battery/
Battery status, capacity (0\-100%), voltage, current, power, and static
design energy (read-only).
.IP
PMU protocol v2 status-report energy values are not trusted as live or measured
battery energy:
.B energy_full
is the static
.B energy-full-design-microwatt-hours
value from the
.B simple-battery
device-tree node, not PMU-measured full capacity;
.B energy_now
is not exported by current releases.
.B power_now
is computed from PMU voltage and current, not from PMU energy fields.
.TP
.B /sys/class/power_supply/battery/charge_control_end_threshold
Charge stop threshold in percent (read-write, 50\-100). The PMU stores and
enforces the threshold, so it survives driver reload and reboot. Values
outside the range are rejected with
.B EINVAL
before any command is sent, a PMU refusal returns
.BR EIO ,
and firmware without charge threshold support returns
.BR ETIMEDOUT .
Raw
.B CHARGE_THRESHOLD_SET
frames written to this device are not forwarded, so the exported value cannot
drift from the PMU state; use this attribute instead.
.TP
.B /sys/class/power_supply/charger/
Charger online status and input voltage (read-only).
.SS Real-Time Clock & Scheduled Boot
.TP
.B /dev/rtc0
Real-time clock backed by PMU. Supports RTC alarms for scheduled
power-on. When an alarm is set, the driver sends the alarm time to
the PMU via UART (SCHEDULE_STARTUP_TIME_SET). The PMU stores this
and will power on the board at the specified time, even when fully
powered off. The alarm is one-shot (non-recurring). Standard Linux
RTC tools such as
.BR rtcwake (8)
work through this interface.
.IP
All firmware starts in
.BR pending-probe ;
the device remains registered, but RTC reads report invalid data and alarm
programming fails until the driver observes three consecutive valid, advancing
PMU RTC samples and promotes the capability to
.BR enabled-probe .
No firmware version string enables RTC or scheduled boot by itself.
Raw scheduled-boot commands sent through
.I /dev/pcat-pm-ctl
are gated by the same RTC capability state.
.SS Sensors & Fan
.TP
.B /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/
Motherboard temperature as a kernel thermal zone. Only present when
.B #thermal-sensor-cells = <0>
is set in the
.B pcat-pm
device tree node and a
.B thermal-zones
binding references it. When present, the kernel thermal governor can
automatically drive the fan cooling device based on board temperature.
.TP
.B /sys/class/thermal/cooling_device*/
Fan control via the thermal cooling device whose
.B type
attribute is
.BR pcat\-pm\-fan .
Cooling-device indexes are not stable; discover the device by type before
writing
.BR cur_state .
Values 0\-100 set fan speed percentage.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/fan_state
Fan management state (read-only). Returns the managed fan speed percentage
(0\-100) once the driver has set one, otherwise
.BR unmanaged .
.IP
No tested firmware exposes a trusted API to reset fan control back to PMU auto
speed, so
.B unmanaged
only means the driver has not sent a fan SET command since loading: the PMU may
still be holding a fixed speed set before a reboot. After a shutdown in managed
fan speed the fan stays at the last fixed speed and will not adjust on its own.
A low retained speed on a device that is still charging or otherwise thermally
active is unsafe.
.SS LEDs & Peripherals
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/status_led
Status LED control (read-write). Write 1 to enable, 0 to disable.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/beeper
Beeper control (read-write). Write 1 to enable, 0 to disable.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/net_status_led_on_time
Network status LED on time in milliseconds (read-write, 0\-65535).
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/net_status_led_off_time
Network status LED off time in milliseconds (read-write, 0\-65535).
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/net_status_led_repeat
Network status LED repeat count (read-write, 0\-65535). 0 = infinite.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/movement_trigger
Accelerometer-based motion detection (read-only). Returns 1 if motion
detected, 0 otherwise.
.SS Power Button
The PMU announces a power button press with a PMU_REQUEST_SHUTDOWN frame.
The
.B pmu-button-mode
device tree property decides what the driver does with it: power the system
off itself, report
.B KEY_POWER
on an input device named \(dqphotonicat-pm power button\(dq, or ignore the
press. See DEVICE TREE below.
.PP
The button is wired to the PMU, not to the SoC. The
.B rk805 pwrkey
input device that a Photonicat 2 also exposes belongs to the PMIC and is a
different button, so
.B HandlePowerKey=
in
.BR logind.conf (5)
has no effect on the PMU button unless
.B pmu-button-mode
is set to \(dqinput\(dq.
.PP
The PMU sends one frame per press and never announces a release, so the
driver reports a press immediately followed by a release. Userspace sees
every press as a short press;
.B HandlePowerKeyLongPress=
cannot trigger from this button.
.PP
The firmware debounces the button itself: on RA2E1260702000 a quick tap or
a hold under about two seconds sends nothing, and a hold of about three
seconds sends the request.
.PP
The PMU applies
.B force-poweroff-timeout
to a shutdown it announces itself, not only to one the host announces.
Once it has sent PMU_REQUEST_SHUTDOWN it stops reporting status and cuts
power that many seconds later whatever the host does; on RA2E1260702000
that was 62 seconds with the property set to 60 and 125 seconds with it set
to 120.
.PP
So that a press the host declines does not become a power cut, the driver
sends 0 for that timeout while the system is running in \(dqinput\(dq or
\(dqignore\(dq mode, and restores the configured value in the shutdown
handler. A press then leaves the PMU running normally, and a shutdown that
hangs is still cut short. A kernel hang is caught by the 60 s heartbeat
watchdog in every mode. A hung userspace is not: in \(dqinput\(dq mode the
button then only queues an event nobody reads, and the firmware has no
hard-cutoff hold, so recovery is SysRq, the serial console, or
disconnecting power; \(dqpoweroff\(dq mode forces the shutdown from the
kernel instead.
.SS PMU Information
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/pmu_hw_version
PMU hardware version string (read-only). Queried from PMU on driver load.
The raw version acknowledgment frame is also forwarded to
.IR /dev/pcat-pm-ctl .
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/pmu_fw_version
PMU firmware version string (read-only). Queried from PMU on driver load.
The raw version acknowledgment frame is also forwarded to
.IR /dev/pcat-pm-ctl ,
which allows tools such as
.B pcat-pmu-updater
.B --pmu-fw-version-get
to read the response through the control device.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/pmu_rtc_capability
PMU RTC policy state (read-only). Values:
.B pending-probe
or
.BR enabled-probe .
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/pmu_charge_threshold_capability
PMU charge threshold policy state (read-only). Values:
.B pending-probe
or
.BR enabled-probe .
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/pmu_power_on_mode_capability
PMU power-on mode policy state (read-only). Values:
.B pending-probe
or
.BR enabled-probe .
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/power_on_event
Last power-on event code (read-only). Values: 0 = unknown,
1 = power button, 2 = scheduled, 3 = charger connected, 4 = USB.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/charger_on_auto_start
Charger auto-start control (read-write). Write 1 to enable automatic
startup when charger is connected, 0 to disable.
.TP
.B /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/power_on_mode
PMU power-on mode (read-write). Reads
.BR unconfigured ,
.BR enabled ,
or
.BR disabled ;
accepts
.B enabled
/
.B 1
and
.B disabled
/
.BR 0 .
The PMU stores the mode.
.B unconfigured
is the state of a PMU whose mode has never been set, and leaving it is one-way:
the PMU accepts only
.B enabled
and
.BR disabled ,
so the first write leaves it permanently. Raw
.B POWER_ON_MODE_V2_SET
frames written to
.I /dev/pcat-pm-ctl
are forwarded only when they carry the query payload
.RB ( 0xFF );
raw writes are blocked so the exported state cannot drift from the PMU.
.PP
The PMU voltage threshold command
.RB ( VOLTAGE_THRESHOLD_SET ", " 0x17 )
is not implemented. The PMU refuses it for every payload tested and has no
command to read the thresholds back. The command number remains in
.I photonicat-pm.h
for raw control-device users.
.SH DEVICE TREE
The driver reads configuration from the device tree node at probe time.
.SS Required Properties
.TP
.B compatible
Must be \(dqphotonicat-pm\(dq.
.SS Optional Properties
.TP
.B power-gpio
GPIO pin wired to PMU power-sense input (active high). Pulled low at
shutdown to signal the PMU. Get the pin from the board schematic; omit
if no such wire exists.
.TP
.B baudrate
Serial port baud rate. Must match the PMU firmware's configured speed.
Default: 115200.
.TP
.B force-poweroff-timeout
Forced power off timeout in seconds (0\-255). Sent to the PMU via
WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_SET command at driver probe. When non-zero, the PMU cuts
power this many seconds after a shutdown is announced \(em by the host, and
also by the PMU itself when the power button is pressed.
The 60 s heartbeat watchdog does not cap it: that one fires only when
heartbeats stop, and 120 here measured a 125 s cut.
Outside
.B pmu-button-mode
\(dqpoweroff\(dq the driver sends 0 while the system is running and the
configured value at shutdown, so a declined button press is not a power
cut. When set to 0, only the heartbeat watchdog guards against hangs.
Default: 0 (disabled).
.TP
.B pmu-button-mode
What the driver does when the PMU reports a power button press.
.RS
.TP
.B "\(dqpoweroff\(dq"
Call
.BR orderly_poweroff (9)
from the driver. This is what every release before this property existed
did, and stays the default so out-of-tree device trees keep their behavior.
.TP
.B "\(dqinput\(dq"
Report
.B KEY_POWER
press and release on an input device and take no other action. Userspace
owns the policy, including whether to power off at all.
.TP
.B "\(dqignore\(dq"
Log the press and do nothing.
.RE
.IP
An unrecognized value falls back to \(dqpoweroff\(dq with a warning.
Overridden by the
.B button_mode
module parameter when that is set (see MODULE PARAMETERS).
Default: \(dqpoweroff\(dq.
.TP
.B pm-version
PMU protocol version (1 or 2). Determined by the PMU firmware on the
board. Version 2 adds battery current and PMU-reported capacity.
Default: 1.
.TP
.B #thermal-sensor-cells
Must be
.B <0>
when present. Exposes the motherboard temperature to the kernel thermal
framework so that a
.B thermal-zones
binding in the board DTS can reference this node via
.BR thermal-sensors .
Without this property the driver still registers an hwmon sensor but no
thermal zone.
.SH MODULE PARAMETERS
.TP
.B button_mode
Power button mode: \(dqpoweroff\(dq, \(dqinput\(dq, or \(dqignore\(dq
(see the
.B pmu-button-mode
device tree property above for what each mode does). When set, it overrides
the device tree property, which avoids a device tree overlay and a reboot
when trying a mode out. An unrecognized value is ignored with a warning and
the device tree property (or the default) applies. The driver logs the mode
it settled on and where it came from at probe.
Default: unset.
.SH HWMON SENSORS
The driver registers hardware monitoring devices accessible via
.BR sensors (1):
.TP
.B pcat_pm_hwmon_temp_mb-*
Motherboard temperature sensor.
.TP
.B pcat_pm_hwmon_speed_fan-*
Fan speed in RPM.
.PP
The motherboard temperature is also registered as a kernel thermal zone
(see
.B /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/
above) when the device tree provides the required binding.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Check that live energy is not exported:
.B test ! \-e /sys/class/power_supply/battery/energy_now
.TP
Stop charging at 80%:
.B echo 80 > /sys/class/power_supply/battery/charge_control_end_threshold
.TP
Set fan to 50% from bash, zsh, or fish:
.RS
.nf
sudo sh \-c '
speed=$1
fan_cdev=
for cdev in /sys/class/thermal/cooling_device*; do
    [ "$(cat "$cdev/type" 2>/dev/null)" = "pcat\-pm\-fan" ] || continue
    fan_cdev=$cdev
    break
done
[ \-n "$fan_cdev" ] || { echo "pcat\-pm\-fan cooling device not found" >&2; exit 1; }
echo "$speed" > "$fan_cdev/cur_state"
\&' sh 50
.fi
.RE
.TP
Turn the status LED off, then read the state the PMU acknowledged:
.B "echo 0 > /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/status_led; sleep 1; cat /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/status_led"
.TP
Set net LED blink pattern (50 ms on, 50 ms off, repeating):
.B "echo 50 > /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/net_status_led_on_time && echo 50 > /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/net_status_led_off_time && echo 0 > /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/net_status_led_repeat"
.TP
Power off now, automatically power on after 60 seconds:
.B rtcwake -m off -s 60
.TP
Power off now, automatically power on at the specified time:
.B rtcwake -m off -t $(date -d \(dq2026-02-11 08:00:00\(dq +%s)
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/pcat-pm-ctl
Raw PMU command interface
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/photonicat-pm/
Sysfs attributes
.TP
.I /dev/input/by-path/platform-*.serial-event
Power button input device, present only in \(dqinput\(dq mode. The by-path
name comes from the UART address, so it is board-specific;
.I platform-2afc0000.serial-event
on the Photonicat 2.
.SH DEBUGGING
PMU command logging uses the kernel
.BR dev_dbg (9)
facility. Messages are off by default.
.SS Runtime (Dynamic Debug)
If the kernel has
.B CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG
enabled (most distributions do):
.PP
.RS
.nf
# Enable PMU command logs
echo 'file pcat\-pm\-uart.c +p' > /sys/kernel/debug/dynamic_debug/control

# Disable
echo 'file pcat\-pm\-uart.c \-p' > /sys/kernel/debug/dynamic_debug/control

# View logs
dmesg | grep "PMU cmd="
.fi
.RE
.SS Build Time
Add
.B \-DDEBUG
to the module CFLAGS in
.I src/Makefile
to enable all
.BR dev_dbg (9)
messages unconditionally:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccflags\-y += \-DDEBUG
.fi
.RE
